1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,250
Two minutes past eight,
on a Monday morning.

2
00:00:08,333 --> 00:00:10,952
A man swings a sledgehammer at a window.

3
00:00:11,035 --> 00:00:12,333
It bounces.

4
00:00:12,416 --> 00:00:14,868
Another man charges it
with a battering ram.

5
00:00:14,951 --> 00:00:17,110
Seventy kilos.

6
00:00:17,193 --> 00:00:18,907
Nothing.

7
00:00:18,990 --> 00:00:22,632
The man who planned this had said
the glass would break like a cracker.

8
00:00:22,715 --> 00:00:25,599
He had reportedly
given it fifteen seconds.

9
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,583
Stavanger. Norway's oil city.

10
00:00:47,666 --> 00:00:51,440
Behind the old cathedral square,
below street level,

11
00:00:51,523 --> 00:00:53,940
was a room
where the region's cash was counted.

12
00:00:54,023 --> 00:00:58,018
In two thousand and four,
the company that ran it,

13
00:00:58,102 --> 00:01:01,201
NOKAS, was about to move
somewhere far more secure.

14
00:01:01,285 --> 00:01:04,581
For one man, that meant now, or never.

15
00:01:10,165 --> 00:01:11,911
His name was David Toska.

16
00:01:11,994 --> 00:01:14,908
In two thousand and three,
he moved to Sandnes,

17
00:01:14,992 --> 00:01:19,045
just south of the city,
with his partner and their newborn son.

18
00:01:19,128 --> 00:01:22,288
As a teenager,
he had been a talented chess player.

19
00:01:22,371 --> 00:01:24,103
He thought in moves.

20
00:01:24,186 --> 00:01:26,611
And every board needs a way in.

21
00:01:31,263 --> 00:01:33,650
The way in came
from a young security guard.

22
00:01:33,734 --> 00:01:35,168
Twenty-six years old.

23
00:01:35,251 --> 00:01:37,442
He met Toska several times.

24
00:01:37,526 --> 00:01:39,147
He described the courtyard.

25
00:01:39,231 --> 00:01:40,466
He drew the rooms.

26
00:01:40,550 --> 00:01:42,763
And he talked about a window.

27
00:01:42,846 --> 00:01:44,749
The glass into the basement.

28
00:01:44,832 --> 00:01:46,985
He says he thought it was a burglary.

29
00:01:47,068 --> 00:01:48,676
He got three years.

30
00:01:50,344 --> 00:01:52,361
From a small atrium in that courtyard,

31
00:01:52,444 --> 00:01:55,364
you could see straight
into the counting room.

32
00:01:55,448 --> 00:01:58,231
One of the robbers later described it.

33
00:01:58,314 --> 00:02:00,702
"You see the vaults being opened at eight.

34
00:02:00,785 --> 00:02:02,592
You see pallets of money,

35
00:02:02,675 --> 00:02:05,050
and you know there are
hundreds of millions

36
00:02:05,133 --> 00:02:07,594
there." Between him and the money,

37
00:02:07,677 --> 00:02:09,664
there was one pane of glass.

38
00:02:15,379 --> 00:02:17,915
Monday, the second of February.

39
00:02:17,998 --> 00:02:21,774
Toska lay in that courtyard,
freezing, waiting.

40
00:02:21,858 --> 00:02:24,644
And the robbery was called off.

41
00:02:24,727 --> 00:02:26,398
Why depends on who you ask.

42
00:02:26,481 --> 00:02:29,389
One account says he saw
the money being moved out.

43
00:02:29,472 --> 00:02:32,047
Another says the radios failed.

44
00:02:32,130 --> 00:02:34,855
Four days later, everything changed.

45
00:02:36,105 --> 00:02:40,082
On the sixth of February, in Oslo,
Toska's best friend was shot dead.

46
00:02:40,165 --> 00:02:42,504
His name was William Ellingsen.

47
00:02:42,588 --> 00:02:43,924
He was twenty-nine.

48
00:02:44,007 --> 00:02:47,112
And he had helped plan the robbery.

49
00:02:47,195 --> 00:02:49,529
A stray bullet, through a door.

50
00:02:49,612 --> 00:02:51,901
He was not the target.

51
00:02:51,984 --> 00:02:55,565
At the funeral, Toska gave the eulogy.

52
00:02:55,649 --> 00:02:57,806
Afterwards, the plan changed.

53
00:02:57,889 --> 00:03:00,193
It had to be spectacular, he decided.

54
00:03:00,277 --> 00:03:02,230
Massive. More men.

55
00:03:02,313 --> 00:03:06,417
One of them later said William's death led
to irrational planning.

56
00:03:06,500 --> 00:03:11,429
He wanted the robbery
to be a monument to his friend.

57
00:03:11,513 --> 00:03:13,827
It would become a monument.

58
00:03:13,911 --> 00:03:15,449
Just not to William.

59
00:03:23,802 --> 00:03:27,831
They rehearsed the escape,
on footpaths and unfinished cycle paths.

60
00:03:27,915 --> 00:03:30,425
They gathered mattresses,
bedding and towels,

61
00:03:30,508 --> 00:03:32,584
to soak in fuel and burn inside a truck.

62
00:03:32,667 --> 00:03:35,779
Fire would take care of the evidence.

63
00:03:35,863 --> 00:03:38,849
Monday, the fifteenth of March.

64
00:03:38,932 --> 00:03:40,307
A second attempt.

65
00:03:40,390 --> 00:03:41,889
A red alarm light.

66
00:03:41,972 --> 00:03:43,483
A guard.

67
00:03:43,566 --> 00:03:44,897
Called off again.

68
00:03:49,724 --> 00:03:51,129
They chose a third date.

69
00:03:51,212 --> 00:03:53,430
Monday, the fifth of April.

70
00:03:53,513 --> 00:03:55,083
Easter week.

71
00:03:55,166 --> 00:03:57,984
Many police officers on holiday.

72
00:03:58,067 --> 00:04:00,211
But the police had heard something.

73
00:04:00,294 --> 00:04:02,469
Warnings. A working group.

74
00:04:02,552 --> 00:04:05,417
A thirteen-page intelligence document.

75
00:04:05,501 --> 00:04:07,205
On Friday, the second of April,

76
00:04:07,288 --> 00:04:10,541
revolvers went into three
patrol cars instead of two.

77
00:04:10,625 --> 00:04:13,391
The warning did not reach
everyone it should have.

78
00:04:13,474 --> 00:04:16,924
An official inquiry later
called it a system failure.

79
00:04:17,008 --> 00:04:21,030
One of the men who did read it
was Arne Sigve Klungland.

80
00:04:21,114 --> 00:04:22,686
Fifty-three.

81
00:04:22,770 --> 00:04:24,833
Thirty years in the police.

82
00:04:24,916 --> 00:04:27,027
Dog handler. Rescue leader.

83
00:04:27,111 --> 00:04:29,241
He trained police students.

84
00:04:29,324 --> 00:04:33,129
And on church trips, he was the one
who drove the pensioners' bus.

85
00:04:36,802 --> 00:04:38,986
Half past three in the morning.

86
00:04:39,070 --> 00:04:42,765
A van collected them from Toska's
townhouse and drove them to the woods.

87
00:04:42,848 --> 00:04:46,753
Before they left, they taped
white bands onto their overalls.

88
00:04:46,836 --> 00:04:48,245
Mourning bands.

89
00:04:48,328 --> 00:04:50,104
For William.

90
00:04:50,188 --> 00:04:51,489
No battle cry.

91
00:04:51,573 --> 00:04:52,934
Just silence.

92
00:05:00,243 --> 00:05:02,586
That morning, at the police briefing,

93
00:05:02,669 --> 00:05:06,103
it was Klungland who passed
the warning on to the patrols.

94
00:05:06,186 --> 00:05:09,379
One of the officers,
Erik Håland, said later:

95
00:05:09,463 --> 00:05:13,626
"That's when I understood
this was serious." Two rooms,

96
00:05:13,709 --> 00:05:16,297
getting ready for the same morning.

97
00:05:21,145 --> 00:05:22,399
Seven-forty.

98
00:05:22,483 --> 00:05:25,945
Security cameras show eleven men,
dressed and armed.

99
00:05:26,029 --> 00:05:27,664
Eight o'clock.

100
00:05:27,747 --> 00:05:32,063
A white truck is driven into the police
station's driveway and set alight.

101
00:05:32,146 --> 00:05:34,064
Tear gas at the entrance.

102
00:05:35,480 --> 00:05:37,994
Two minutes,
fifty-eight seconds past eight.

103
00:05:38,078 --> 00:05:40,684
The police register the alarm.

104
00:05:40,768 --> 00:05:43,193
Toska fired at the glass himself.

105
00:05:43,276 --> 00:05:44,940
Then he fetched Schumann,

106
00:05:45,024 --> 00:05:50,153
who emptied magazine after magazine
along the edge of the window.

107
00:05:50,237 --> 00:05:52,892
One hundred and thirteen shots.

108
00:06:10,480 --> 00:06:14,586
Below them, three women who worked there
pushed two trolleys into the lift.

109
00:06:14,669 --> 00:06:17,927
On the trolleys: sixty million kroner.

110
00:06:18,011 --> 00:06:21,255
As the doors closed,
they could hear the gunfire.

111
00:06:21,338 --> 00:06:25,071
Upstairs, the window finally gave way.

112
00:06:25,155 --> 00:06:26,175
They were in.

113
00:06:26,259 --> 00:06:28,370
It was eight minutes past eight.

114
00:06:28,454 --> 00:06:30,725
Six minutes behind the plan.

115
00:06:30,809 --> 00:06:35,217
And outside, the first police car had just
turned onto the cathedral square.

116
00:06:40,862 --> 00:06:44,806
They had brought fifteen bags,
for three or four hundred million kroner.

117
00:06:44,890 --> 00:06:48,519
What they found was mostly
blue two-hundred-kroner notes.

118
00:06:48,602 --> 00:06:51,917
There was a barred gate to the main vault.

119
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:53,450
They hit it with the ram.

120
00:06:53,534 --> 00:06:55,334
It didn't move.

121
00:06:55,418 --> 00:06:58,751
Toska ran from room to room
with a watch in his hand.

122
00:06:58,834 --> 00:07:00,752
The plan said eight minutes.

123
00:07:00,836 --> 00:07:03,159
It was going to take nearly twenty.

124
00:07:07,385 --> 00:07:11,137
Outside, officer Erik Håland
and his partner had revolvers.

125
00:07:11,221 --> 00:07:14,266
Twelve rounds each.
Against them, military rifles.

126
00:07:14,350 --> 00:07:19,076
At a street corner, Schumann forced
a plain-clothes officer to the ground.

127
00:07:19,159 --> 00:07:22,778
"Who are you?" And all around them,
the city kept going.

128
00:07:22,861 --> 00:07:26,266
A bus. People on their way to work.

129
00:07:26,349 --> 00:07:27,949
Women pushing prams.

130
00:07:28,032 --> 00:07:29,900
Some thought it was an exercise.

131
00:07:29,984 --> 00:07:31,986
Fourteen minutes past eight.

132
00:07:32,069 --> 00:07:33,735
Shooting across the square.

133
00:07:33,819 --> 00:07:36,474
One of the robbers was hit in the leg.

134
00:07:42,305 --> 00:07:43,472
More police came.

135
00:07:43,556 --> 00:07:45,905
Among them, the command vehicle.

136
00:07:45,989 --> 00:07:50,853
In it, Arne Sigve Klungland,
driving toward the shooting.

137
00:07:50,936 --> 00:07:52,585
Seventeen minutes past eight.

138
00:07:52,668 --> 00:07:54,155
He tried to reverse.

139
00:07:54,238 --> 00:07:56,269
There was a bus behind him.

140
00:08:02,019 --> 00:08:04,024
Arne Sigve Klungland was killed there.

141
00:08:04,107 --> 00:08:06,240
He was fifty-three.

142
00:08:19,647 --> 00:08:24,030
At twenty past eight, the last car
left the square, still firing.

143
00:08:24,114 --> 00:08:28,321
In the woods at Sørmarka, they set
the cars on fire and drove on in others.

144
00:08:28,404 --> 00:08:31,396
The fire was supposed to erase them.

145
00:08:31,479 --> 00:08:32,540
It didn't.

146
00:08:32,623 --> 00:08:38,259
The truck barely burned,
and the fire station was right next door.

147
00:08:38,342 --> 00:08:42,053
Later that day, six of them sat
in a flat on Nedre Dalgate,

148
00:08:42,136 --> 00:08:44,790
a few hundred metres from the square,
with the money.

149
00:08:44,873 --> 00:08:49,285
On the television, they learned
that a police officer was dead.

150
00:08:57,341 --> 00:08:59,083
On the fourteenth of April,

151
00:08:59,167 --> 00:09:03,100
about six hundred police officers came
to Stavanger Cathedral.

152
00:09:03,183 --> 00:09:05,928
A helicopter flew low over the city.

153
00:09:06,011 --> 00:09:09,976
Arne Sigve Klungland had been married
since nineteen seventy-seven.

154
00:09:10,060 --> 00:09:12,350
He left a wife and two children.

155
00:09:20,221 --> 00:09:23,058
In the counting room,
the police found an earplug.

156
00:09:23,142 --> 00:09:25,293
Toska had lost it.

157
00:09:25,376 --> 00:09:26,541
Inside was his DNA.

158
00:09:26,624 --> 00:09:30,198
And the barred gate to the main vault,

159
00:09:30,281 --> 00:09:34,043
the one they had battered and
could not break, had never been locked.

160
00:09:34,126 --> 00:09:38,572
They could have reached
in and lifted the handle.

161
00:09:38,655 --> 00:09:41,137
Downstairs,
three women had saved sixty million.

162
00:09:41,221 --> 00:09:43,380
More than the robbers took.

163
00:09:49,676 --> 00:09:54,553
Toska ran. To a village in southern Spain,
with his partner and their baby.

164
00:09:54,636 --> 00:09:58,035
In Oslo, a police data
specialist they called

165
00:09:58,119 --> 00:10:00,566
the Sharp Knife watched
the internet cafés.

166
00:10:00,649 --> 00:10:04,995
They found his email address:
omaha beach, at postmaster.

167
00:10:05,078 --> 00:10:09,609
In Madrid, Spanish police reached
a café five minutes too late.

168
00:10:09,693 --> 00:10:12,331
One reporter later called it a chess game.

169
00:10:12,414 --> 00:10:16,985
The Sharp Knife, against the man
the press called the Mastermind.

170
00:10:21,338 --> 00:10:24,414
The fifth of April, two thousand and five.

171
00:10:24,498 --> 00:10:26,401
One year to the day.

172
00:10:26,485 --> 00:10:28,538
At the request of the Norwegian police,

173
00:10:28,621 --> 00:10:31,446
his email provider slowed
logins across Europe.

174
00:10:31,530 --> 00:10:35,667
Toska stayed online longer than usual,
reading news from home,

175
00:10:35,751 --> 00:10:38,281
about the memorial
for Arne Sigve Klungland.

176
00:10:38,365 --> 00:10:41,428
In Stavanger, that memorial was under way.

177
00:10:41,512 --> 00:10:44,491
In Málaga, police walked into the café.

178
00:10:44,574 --> 00:10:49,617
In Stavanger, the news went through
the gathering like a murmur.

179
00:10:53,352 --> 00:10:55,488
Thirteen men were convicted.

180
00:10:55,571 --> 00:10:58,860
Schumann admitted firing the shots
that killed Klungland.

181
00:10:58,943 --> 00:11:00,879
He says it was a mistake.

182
00:11:00,963 --> 00:11:03,565
The court found it was an aimed shot.

183
00:11:03,649 --> 00:11:06,428
Toska was sentenced to eighteen years.

184
00:11:07,765 --> 00:11:10,405
About fifty-one million kroner
has never been found.

185
00:11:10,489 --> 00:11:11,978
Ten years later,

186
00:11:12,062 --> 00:11:16,304
thirty-two people who worked at NOKAS
still carried that morning with them.

187
00:11:16,387 --> 00:11:19,374
He had planned for the glass.

188
00:11:19,457 --> 00:11:20,496
The police.

189
00:11:20,580 --> 00:11:26,379
The city. But the one thing that could
never be replaced… was never on his board.

